ading levels that's very scary it is  concerning it's concerning as the  workplace moves increasingly towards  people needing to be able to communicate  very effectively read and comprehend  very effectively on this one there are  things parents can do we see research  showing that when parents read to  toddlers it actually bumps reading  scores at age 15 the other thing we see  is that when parents themselves read for  their own pleasure when they just read  on their own time  it actually bumps kids engagement and  performance and reading so what is the  level how much how much involvement  should parents have on their on their  kids when it comes to their schooling  because I think a lot of parents look at  the school's the homework and say I  can't do that I can't help well they try  it then it's a fight and doesn't go well  well it's their involvement  well not all involvement is equal so  what we do see is we want parent  involvement but parent involvement in  the countries that are ahead of us  actually does tend to look like academic  coaching more time spent working with  your child on homework supporting  homework parent involvement in the u.s.  is often measured in terms of things  like involvement in sports showing up at  school meetings these matter but they  don't necessarily change the test scores  there any good news in this question  there is there is a bright spot in this  this report what we saw in the u.s. is  that children who go to immigrated to  the US were performing at the same level  in reading as children who had not  immigrated to the US as non immigrant  children and this gap is actually bigger  in other countries than it is in the US  and this is true even for immigrant  children who are impoverished so this  piece does speak to the good work being  done in American schools and by American  teachers and that's something we can  take pride in yeah a lot of work to do  at Lisa d'amour thank you it
 

